# The encoder counters the code around them reads as a width, and which
# tests/check-encoder-units.sh therefore holds to being returned to the
# start of a unit before the unit is offered to the target rather than
# after the target has taken it.
#
# A counter is here when something takes it for a quantity of a known
# size: a shift count, a subscript, or a number taken away from a
# constant. Those are the reads that go wrong once a refused byte has
# left the counter running past the unit it counts. A counter read for
# neither -- one that only counts to a line break, or marks a record
# boundary -- is not here, and naming it would bury the ones that are.
#
# Which counters those are is derived from the source. This register is
# what makes the population visible: one that acquires such a read joins
# it as a line to write, and one that loses it -- because the
# arithmetic that read it as a width moved off the field and onto a
# local -- leaves as a line and a count that went down, rather than as
# this check quietly holding one thing fewer.
#
#   bitcnt     Xpost_BitEncBase: bits gathered short of a byte. The
#              drain shifts the buffer down by it and the padding
#              shifts up by eight less it.
#   bpp        Xpost_PredEncFile: bytes between a byte and the one a
#              pixel to its left, taken away from a subscript to reach
#              that neighbour.
#   codewidth  Xpost_LzwEncFile: the current code width, compared
#              against one shifted left by it.
#   n          Xpost_A85EncFile: bytes of the group of four, the tail
#              shifted by eight times four less it.
#              Xpost_RleEncFile: bytes gathered into the literal block,
#              indexing that block.
#   nextcode   Xpost_LzwEncFile: the next string table entry, indexing
#              the suffix, sibling and child tables.
#   pos        Xpost_PredEncFile: bytes gathered into the current row,
#              indexing a buffer holding exactly one row.
#   prefix     Xpost_LzwEncFile: the string built so far, indexing the
#              child table.
#   rowi       Xpost_DctEncFile: bytes of the current scanline,
#              indexing the row buffer.
#   rowpos     Xpost_FaxEncFile: bytes of the current scanline,
#              indexing a buffer holding exactly one row.
#
# The count below is the size of the list, so that retiring an entry is
# two edits and a population that shrank says so with a number.

entries 9

bitcnt
bpp
codewidth
n
nextcode
pos
prefix
rowi
rowpos
